#[navbar]
#[title, center: PACKAGES AND BUILDERS PROGRESS]

#[section, $md:
    This page tracks current implementation status for Moth packages and project builders.
    It also records the important accepted gaps and next extensions for partial package surfaces so
    later work does not need to rediscover them from implementation details.

    The exact-numeric migration is delivered. Package signatures and constants use exact numeric types, Core Math uses F64 and ordinary numeric defaults use I32/F64. These changes add no HTML-Wasm package capabilities. The rows below distinguish delivered support from accepted extensions.

    The @../ (compiler progress matrix) tracks the core language, compiler, memory work and backend
    implementation. Backend implementation progress belongs there even when a project builder consumes
    that backend. This page tracks builder-owned project behaviour and the packages supplied by Core or
    a project builder.

    Accepted deferred surfaces may appear here. Open design questions and outside-scope mechanisms do
    not. Package acquisition, resolver and package-manager sequencing remains roadmap work rather than
    something this matrix designs.

    ## How to read and edit this page

    - Keep package rows focused on the implemented v1 surface and the most useful accepted next gaps.
    - Update a package row while working on that package so deferred extensions do not need to be
      rediscovered later.
    - Keep project-builder rows separate from backend implementation rows.
    - Put user-facing package and builder explanations in `docs/src/docs/packages/**` and the relevant
      project documentation.
    - Put implementation sequencing and unaccepted proposals in `docs/roadmap/roadmap.md`.
    - Use the status labels from this page's legend. Put backend-specific or deliberately deferred nuance
      in the notes column instead of inventing new status labels.
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Status legend

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Status] [: Meaning] ]

        [data:
            [: Supported]
            [: Implemented for the current supported surface and expected to compile, type check and run through its advertised builder/runtime path.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Partial]
            [: A useful subset is implemented, but important common operations, package surfaces or builder behaviour remain incomplete.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Experimental]
            [: Present in the repository, but not part of the Alpha stability promise.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Deferred]
            [: Accepted or design-compatible follow-up work that is not currently implemented.]
        ]
    ]

    ## Coverage legend

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Coverage] [: Meaning] ]

        [data:
            [: Broad]
            [: Multiple focused cases exist, including success and failure coverage.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Targeted]
            [: Dedicated coverage exists, but not across all important edges.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Thin]
            [: Coverage exists indirectly or through only a small number of cases.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: None]
            [: No clear canonical coverage found.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Package model and infrastructure

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Prelude policy]
            [: Supported]
            [: Targeted prelude symbol registration, namespace-alias plumbing and collision rejection coverage.]
            [: Frontend / JS]
            [: The builtin prelude policy registers [$html:<code>io</code>] as a namespace alias to [$html:<code>@core/io</code>] and leaves compiler-owned [$html:<code>Error</code>] as a bare language symbol. Prelude is visibility policy, not a package origin or backing. There is no [$html:<code>@core/prelude</code>] package.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Binding-backed platform packages]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad provider, registry, asset/glue, exact numeric boundary and target-validation coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/html_js_reexport_linking (html_js_reexport_linking) executes a pure Moth facade. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/html_js_binding_runtime_topology (html_js_binding_runtime_topology) combines one/two-hop facades, aliases/namespaces, repeated imports, Call/Invoke, caught failure, full-width U32, executed generics and distinct same-basename providers. Inactive/private and constant-only controls emit no provider assets. Typed JS parser failures retain the authored Moth import span, and privacy/missing-symbol negatives retain their rejection contracts.]
            [: Frontend / JS]
            [$md: Binding-backed packages expose free functions, opaque types and compile-time scalar constants. Annotated JavaScript providers accept I32/I64/U32/U64/F32/F64 numeric slots alongside Bool, String, Char and same-file opaque handles, as pinned by @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/src/projects/html_project/external_js/tests/package_registration_tests.rs (package registration tests). Flat literal U32 constants form the [$html:<code>@moth.const</code>] subset. Signatures allow zero or one success result with an optional final Error! channel. Shared-type imports, nested annotations, collections, borrowed views, callbacks and async remain unavailable. Constant-only dependencies add semantic inputs without assets. HTML-Wasm rejects unsupported reachable foreign calls before lowering.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Source-backed package roots]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted AST-backed config loading, known-key and shape enforcement, self-contained config dependency rejection, generic package-root discovery, collision detection and public-surface enforcement coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HTML]
            [: Source-backed packages and regular modules share module-root and visibility rules. [$html:<code>config.moth</code>] is compiled as one self-contained source before package discovery and rejects every dependency clause before path resolution.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builder-provided source-backed packages]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted builder source-backed registration and HTML Builder package coverage. Direct-file and directory consumers share HTML/Canvas package boundaries and reject registered-package source collisions, including case-only names and child scopes. Missing direct-file dependencies retain authored spans, and ordinary/Boracle package failures retain source context. No-dependency direct-file consumers skip package bodies but validate namespaces. Template file values retain implicit HTML helpers. Nested and flat registered-package graphs exercise the real writer and Node with distinct logical JS paths, exact provider bytes, runtime values and stable import/wrapper order across repeated and reversed creation/registration.]
            [: Frontend / HTML]
            [$md: Builders expose source-backed packages through [$html:<code>BuilderSurface.source_packages</code>], including HTML's [$html:<code>@html</code>]. Registered-provider fixtures use normal [$html:<code>@mod.moth</code>] roots, without changing structural [$html:<code>+*.moth</code>] support packages. Builder/project-local prefix collisions remain hard config errors. Distinct project/package providers at the same boundary-relative path still collide independently. The @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/blob/main/docs/roadmap/evidence/js-import-linking-and-failure-reporting-review.md#independent-provider-identity-collision (retained collision report) records the stable-package-identity handoff needed next. General cross-package provider-prefix support remains incomplete.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Project-local packages]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted structural support-package roots, rejected legacy package-folder configuration, independently registered builder packages, dependency resolution, generic normal module-root and public-surface coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HTML]
            [: Structural [$html:<code>+*.moth</code>] support packages and the optional project-root facade define the accepted project-local package model. Legacy config-driven [$html:<code>package_folders</code>] paths and the historical default [$html:<code>lib/</code>] are removed. Independently registered Builder/Core source packages retain their own package boundaries.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Public Rust builder SDK]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted definition registration, rejection and external-crate compile coverage.]
            [: Frontend / Rust]
            [$md: [$html:<code>moth::builder::BuilderDefinition</code>] registers Builder-origin source packages, native host packages described with [$html:<code>moth::host::HostFunction</code>] and opt-in [$html:<code>.mtf</code>]/[$html:<code>.md</code>] source kinds through the existing registries. [$html:<code>finish</code>] rejects invalid builder identities, duplicate or overlapping package paths, reserved [$html:<code>@project</code>] and [$html:<code>@core</code>] paths and host signatures outside the V1 profile. Building a project from a definition, builder configuration sections, output publication and native Wasm emission are not available yet.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builder runtime packages]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted built-in JS-backed package, runtime asset, runtime module, pure-facade import-map, glue and backend validation coverage. Import-map assertions inspect emitted artefacts separately from the normal Node harness, which rejects import maps.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [: Builder-owned runtime packages such as [$html:<code>@web/canvas</code>] are binding-backed packages. They share the specialised external package registry and HTML external JS asset/glue pipeline with provider-created imports.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: First-party no-third-party-dependencies guard]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted package-manager, vendored-root, allowed-runtime-import and rejected-module-import coverage.]
            [: Validation / packages]
            [: The [$html:<code>first-party-deps</code>] validation owner inspects first-party implementation roots plus the compiler-owned JavaScript inventory. Physical [$html:<code>.js</code>] assets, Core helper bodies, inline lowering templates and registered runtime modules are checked. User-owned and future dependency packages, generated glue, documentation, tests and benchmarks remain outside its scan.]
        ]
    ]
]


#[section, $md:
    ## Core packages

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes and accepted next gaps] ]

        [data:
            [: Core IO package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad console helpers, prelude/explicit namespace dependencies, old callable rejection, removed public IO, input metadata/type-checking, HTML-JS input helper reachability and backend unsupported-call diagnostics. Executable Node tests with a fake browser host cover snapshot publication, press/release transitions, repeat suppression, coalescing, [$html:<code>last_*</code>] reads, modifier-safe key release, focus and visibility release, teardown and bounded storage in [$html:<code>src/backends/js/tests/io_input_runtime.rs</code>]. An integration case observes the [$html:<code>Unsupported</code>] code 1 from [$html:<code>io.input.new</code>] on a host without input.]
            [: Frontend / JS / HTML-Wasm validation]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/io</code>] exposes [$html:<code>io.print</code>], [$html:<code>io.line</code>], [$html:<code>io.debug</code>], [$html:<code>io.warn</code>], [$html:<code>io.error</code>] and [$html:<code>io.input.*</code>]. The prelude injects [$html:<code>io</code>] as a compile-time namespace alias, not a runtime value. It is the broad host-capability facade and the only preluded Core package. Input polling follows the published snapshot contract: [$html:<code>update</code>] is the only publication point, transitions coalesce per key and button, focus loss releases held input and [$html:<code>close</code>] leaves the handle inert. HTML-JS emits [$html:<code>print</code>] and [$html:<code>line</code>] as one browser console entry each. Portable snapshot additions are the next candidates. An ordered event queue waits for a final Moth event value that crosses the binding boundary. Timers, sleep, event waits, network events and callbacks wait for structured async and a host-ingress contract. Targeted browser event control belongs to future [$html:<code>@web/*</code>] surfaces. Queued [$html:<code>io.set_title</code>] is a later host-title call, not current IO support; see the deferred row below.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core collections package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad literal, fixed/growable mutation, indexing, fallibility, AST/HIR call target, JS helper and diagnostic coverage.]
            [: Frontend / JS / HTML-Wasm validation]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/collections</code>] provides the compiler-owned collection operation identities behind fixed and growable collection members. Common non-sorting gaps still need a focused package audit. The accepted stable-by-default sorting contract is owned by its specialised package plan and remains blocked on final mixed-backend collection support.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core math package]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Broad runtime and diagnostic coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_math_basic_functions (core_math_basic_functions) and transcendental fixtures cover all thirty-one registered functions. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_math_constants (core_math_constants) checks all six nearest F64 constants. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_math_float_boundary_validation (core_math_float_boundary_validation) covers domain and finite-result failures.]
            [: JS / HTML-Wasm validation]
            [$md: @../../packages/core/math (Core Math) owns the delivered F64 function and constant contracts, including domains, units and finite-result delivery. Calls require an explicit dependency. HTML-Wasm function lowering and compile-time evaluation of function calls remain unsupported. The six constants remain compile-time values. Richer and generic Math APIs remain accepted later work, not part of this scalar migration.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core text package]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Targeted runtime, Unicode, helper reachability, arity, type-error and compile-time evaluation coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_text_functions (core_text_functions) covers HTML output and HTML-Wasm rejection, with additional negative-predicate edge cases. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_text_runtime_parity (core_text_runtime_parity) pairs folded and runtime results, and @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_text_helper_retention_mixed (core_text_helper_retention_mixed) keeps only the helpers that runtime calls still need.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/text</code>] exposes length, emptiness, containment, prefix and suffix checks. text.length returns I32 and counts Unicode scalar values. Calls whose arguments are compile-time strings fold through compiler-owned Core evaluation, so fully folded calls need no helper and also build on HTML-Wasm. Accepted extensions include scalar-indexed access and slicing, search positions, match counting, Unicode-whitespace trimming and literal replacement. Collection-valued APIs await supported binding signatures. Unicode casing, case folding, normalisation and grapheme operations await a pinned Moth-owned Unicode data policy. Regex and locale-sensitive text remain separate design areas. Runtime HTML-Wasm calls remain target-rejected.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core random package]
            [: Partial]
            [$md: Targeted dependency, helper, runtime, arity and type-error coverage. @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_random_functions (core_random_functions) and @https://github.com/nyejames/moth/tree/main/tests/cases/core_random_int_edge_cases (core_random_int_edge_cases) cover HTML execution and HTML-Wasm rejection.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/random</code>] exposes F64 random_float and inclusive I32 random_int helpers. Seeded random and additional distributions remain deferred. The portable observable contracts do not fix a cross-backend generator algorithm. HTML-Wasm calls remain target-rejected.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core time package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad dependency, direct alias, namespace, helper, runtime, arithmetic, parse-grammar, calendar, offset, renderable-window and overflow coverage, with each rejection case observing the returned error code, plus arity/type diagnostic, old-name rejection, opaque-field rejection and backend validation coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML-Wasm validation]
            [$md: [$html:<code>@core/time</code>] exposes opaque Duration, TimeMark and Timestamp values, clocks, conversions, fallible ISO parsing and rendering and Duration/Timestamp arithmetic. @../../packages/core/time (Core Time) owns epoch, units, grammar and rendering limits. Reading a Duration as F64 exposes numeric overflow through the documented failure contract. Calendar, civil-time and time-zone APIs remain deferred, and HTML-Wasm lowering remains unsupported.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## HTML project builder

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes and accepted next gaps] ]

        [data:
            [: HTML project builds]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad routing, API-only artefact filtering, document shell, metadata, config, output cleanup, external JS glue, page-fragment and resource-output coverage. Facade scenarios check exact provider materialisation and demanded wrappers without inactive assets. Multi-entry checks preserve one shared provider and page-specific wrapper selection.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [: This is the release-critical project builder. Only roots with HTML artefact activity participate in page, runtime asset, resource-output and homepage planning. Backend implementation and HTML-Wasm progress remain on the compiler progress matrix.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Fallible HTML entry and runtime fragment publication]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted synthetic-start, propagated/implicit failure, code-zero failure, local recovery, once-only execution, fragment suppression and safe release-notice coverage. The execution harness records failure at the generated start call.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [$md: Applicable synthetic [$html:<code>start</code>] has builtin [$html:<code>Error!</code>]. The generated caller publishes invocation-owned runtime fragments only after success. Failure preserves static HTML and earlier explicit IO, reports a fixed terminal notice independently of [$html:<code>Error.code</code>] and appends fixed safe release text, not application Error HTML. Wasm capability diagnostics appear in @../ (Compiler Progress). [$html:<code>moth dev</code>] shows entry errors, assertions and unexpected startup faults in a page-local view and reports each invocation once to the terminal.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Moth template [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] content assets]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted compiler-integrated dependency binding, synthetic content constant, markdown folding, module-root re-export, direct API, diagnostics and integration coverage.]
            [: HTML builder / frontend]
            [: [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] files are builder-supported source assets that synthesize one private compile-time [$html:<code>content #String</code>] constant from an implicit [$html:<code>$md</code>] template body. Nested templates without an explicit directive also default to Markdown and explicit directives override that default. They bind extensionlessly into [$html:<code>.moth</code>] and must fully fold.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Plain Markdown [$html:<code>.md</code>] content assets]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad extensionless dependency, direct alias, module-root re-export, parser options, raw HTML, literal links/images, collision, direct-extension and module-root rejection coverage.]
            [: HTML builder / frontend]
            [: [$html:<code>.md</code>] files render to HTML at compile time and synthesize one private [$html:<code>content #String</code>] constant. They do not parse Moth declarations, interpolation, templates, frontmatter or metadata.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builder-supported source file kinds]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted moth template and Markdown source-kind registry, extensionless resolution, unsupported-kind diagnostics, source discovery and tokenizer/preparation coverage.]
            [: Stage 0 / frontend]
            [: Builders can declare non-[$html:<code>.moth</code>] source file kinds that participate in source dependency discovery and frontend preparation. The HTML builder currently implements [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] and plain Markdown [$html:<code>.md</code>] content assets.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: HTML Builder package [$html:<code>@html</code>]]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted source-backed Builder package registration plus docs-site use across templates, style helpers, tables, code blocks and canvas wrappers.]
            [: HTML builder]
            [: [$html:<code>@html</code>] provides compile-time HTML helpers and source-owned wrappers over lower-level builder bindings such as [$html:<code>@web/canvas</code>]. It should grow only where helpers are broadly useful to HTML projects rather than becoming a general web framework.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Canvas package [$html:<code>@web/canvas</code>]]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad binding-backed package registration, opaque handle types, runtime assets/glue and current drawing API coverage, including namespace, alias, missing-symbol and Wasm-unsupported rejection.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [: The current surface covers canvas element management, 2D context state, rectangles, paths, text, gradients, patterns, image drawing, image data and pixel access. Canvas is deliberately allowed a broader expansion target than most packages because visuals and animation make it a useful Moth language and runtime stress-test surface.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Accepted deferred package and builder work

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage / diagnostic direction] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Core Text collection-valued operations]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Keep final collection signatures unavailable rather than exposing opaque JS handles.]
            [: [$html:<code>split</code>], [$html:<code>join</code>], [$html:<code>lines</code>] and an eventual character iteration surface wait for stable collection-valued binding signatures or another final source-backed route.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core Text Unicode transforms]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Exact search and current transforms remain host-version-independent.]
            [: Unicode lowercase/uppercase, case folding and NFC/NFD/NFKC/NFKD normalization wait for one pinned Moth-owned Unicode data policy and generated first-party tables shared across implementations. Host JavaScript or Rust Unicode versions do not define Core semantics.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core Text grapheme operations]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Existing length and positions remain Unicode-scalar based.]
            [: Grapheme-aware length, iteration and slicing wait for an accepted explicit API plus pinned Unicode segmentation data. They do not replace the scalar semantics of [$html:<code>length</code>], [$html:<code>char_at</code>], search positions or [$html:<code>slice</code>].]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core Text fallible constant evaluation]
            [: Deferred]
            [: Runtime APIs keep normal Error semantics; no temporary optional forms are introduced.]
            [: Rust parity functions for fallible text helpers can exist with the package, but compile-time evaluation of [$html:<code>char_at</code>] and [$html:<code>slice</code>] waits for an accepted compiler-owned fallible Core evaluator.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Source-backed package HIR caching]
            [: Deferred]
            [: No precompiled source-backed package cache.]
            [: Source-backed packages are compiled from source each build. Caching stays deferred until package identities and the package system are stable.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Package manager]
            [: Deferred]
            [: No package manager, remote registries, lockfiles or dependency resolver.]
            [: Project dependency declarations, resolver/catalog handoff and package-manager foundations are owned by their later roadmap work. This matrix records status only and does not design that system.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Binding-backed JS package expansion]
            [: Deferred]
            [: JS provider diagnostics reject unsupported public binding forms.]
            [: The HTML builder supports annotated single-file [$html:<code>.js</code>] imports and builder-owned JS-backed packages. Additional binding forms should be added only when their package contract is clear and they fit existing package ownership.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Generic binding-backed package functions]
            [: Deferred]
            [: External signature validation rejects unsupported generic binding-backed surfaces.]
            [: Binding-backed packages currently expose concrete functions, opaque types and constants only. Generic binding-backed functions remain deferred and must not be introduced just to flesh out a package.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: [$html:<code>$html_builder</code>] configuration]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [$md: Closed config-only HTML settings directive with required singleton invocation for authored HTML directory projects. Bare [$html:<code>$html_builder</code>] uses defaults. Current grouped [$html:<code>html</code>] records remain implemented. Compiler-wide [$html:<code>$project</code>] / [$html:<code>$config</code>] status lives in @../ (Progress).]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Root-only [$html:<code>$page</code>] and explicit purpose]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Accepted queued HTML entry declaration. One normal-root [$html:<code>$page</code>] per directory, metadata cutover from reserved [$html:<code>page_*</code>] names, and command-specific no-page rules. Current HTML entries still come from implemented root activity and reserved page metadata.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Dev entry-error reporting]
            [: Supported]
            [: Dev-server and generated-bootstrap tests cover returned Errors including code 0, assertion and unexpected startup-fault categories, hostile text, stale, duplicate, malformed, oversized and cross-origin reports, origin prefixes, unavailable transport and isolated pages. Socket and browser-client tests cover a page catching up after a missed publication broadcast, reconnection to the same generation without reloading and registration interleaved with publication. A release case proves output has no dev reporting dependency.]
            [: In [$html:<code>moth dev</code>], a returned [$html:<code>start</code>] Error covers its page with a runtime view that keeps hot reload, logs to the console and reports once to the server terminal. A successful build stays successful for every page. Assertion failures are identified structurally and other faults escaping the generated startup wrapper are reported as unexpected startup faults. Later callbacks and unrelated scripts are not intercepted. A page that missed a publication reloads when its event stream connects or reconnects.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Browser-capability [$html:<code>io.set_title</code>]]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Queued Core IO call: one String-content input, no value result, HTML-JS host capability, pre-lowering rejection on unsupported reachable targets. Not a silent no-op. Current IO helpers are unchanged.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: WebGL2 package [$html:<code>@web/graphics</code>]]
            [: Deferred]
            [: The HTML builder registers the package with no public symbols. Targeted source cases cover namespace resolution without runtime asset/glue emission and missing-symbol diagnostics at the authored selection. No placeholder or temporary API is published.]
            [: Accepted Builder binding-backed WebGL2 package. WebGL1 is outside current scope. [$html:<code>graphics.gl</code>] is the initial implementation area. [$html:<code>graphics.frame</code>] is accepted scope that waits for an accepted host-to-Moth execution mechanism, never a polling stand-in. The shared binding boundary now supports fixed U32 constants for closed-domain values such as primitive modes, but nested annotated symbol paths, cross-package opaque type references for the [$html:<code>@web/canvas</code>] canvas handle and bulk numeric and byte data transfer remain pending for a useful v1. The package still publishes no symbols and renders nothing.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: MON static project builder]
            [: Deferred]
            [: None]
            [: Accepted late follow-up for compile-time-known [$html:<code>.mon</code>] assets through normal build output ownership. No command, source kind, builder registration, package surface or output writes are implemented. The documented Rust-only codec contract implies none of these.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Maintenance rule

    Keep this page concise but useful as a handoff between package iterations.

    - Record the important common missing operations while a package is being worked on.
    - Move implementation sequencing and unaccepted package ideas to `docs/roadmap/roadmap.md`.
    - Keep backend implementation status on @../ (the compiler progress matrix).
    - Keep package API explanations and examples in `docs/src/docs/packages/**`.
    - Add a new package row only after that package's scope and semantic contract have been deliberately considered.
    - Do not invent package origins or tiers to classify importance. Use only the package model accepted by the canonical package documentation.
]
